  {
   "id": "sci.balance-lcm",
   "name": "Balancing by the LCM method",
   "explain": {
    "en": {
     "text": "In the LCM method, find each total valence (subscript × valence), find the LCM of these numbers, then divide the LCM by each total to get the coefficients. For aluminum and oxygen: totals 3, 4 and 6 give LCM 12, so 4Al + 3O₂ → 2Al₂O₃."
    }
   },
   "screens": [
    {
     "kind": "guess",
     "step": "hook",
     "title": "A common number",
     "ask": "Al has total valence 3, O₂ has 4, Al₂O₃ has 6. What is the smallest number all three divide into?",
     "opts": [
      "6",
      "12",
      "24"
     ],
     "after": "12 is the least common multiple (LCM) of 3, 4 and 6. The LCM method uses it.",
     "id": "g7.science.u3.s3-3-6.11"
    },
    {
     "kind": "steps",
     "step": "method",
     "title": "The LCM method",
     "steps": [
      "Step 1, word equation: aluminum + oxygen → aluminum oxide.",
      "Step 2, formulas: Al + O₂ → Al₂O₃.",
      "Step 3, total valences: Al = 1 × 3 = 3; O₂ = 2 × 2 = 4; Al₂O₃ = 2 × 3 = 6.",
      "Step 4, LCM of 3, 4 and 6 is 12.",
      "Step 5, divide: 12 ÷ 3 = 4 (Al); 12 ÷ 4 = 3 (O₂); 12 ÷ 6 = 2 (Al₂O₃).",
      "4Al + 3O₂ → 2Al₂O₃. Check: Al 4 = 4, O 6 = 6."
     ],
     "id": "g7.science.u3.s3-3-6.12"
    },
    {
     "kind": "name",
     "step": "name",
     "title": "Key words",
     "defs": [
      [
       "LCM method",
       "Balancing using the least common multiple of total valence numbers."
      ]
     ],
     "id": "g7.science.u3.s3-3-6.13"
    },
    {
     "kind": "mcq",
     "step": "check",
